WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.620
PRESENTER 1: I want to know
whether we can teach design

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.840
thinking to a group
of non-designers,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.572
and innovative in
the education system.

00:00:08.572 --> 00:00:10.380
PRESENTER 2: It's
a two day workshop

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.350
where we are learning about
design thinking in order

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.800
to solve real world
education problems.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.660
PRESENTER 3: So
prototypes, like a sketch,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.610
they're just a
representation of an idea.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.610
PRESENTER 4: And when we
think about innovation,

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.710
it's both a
technological innovation,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.422
but it also needs
to be innovation

00:00:25.422 --> 00:00:27.380
around teamwork and
professional ed communities

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.840
and thinking together.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.009
PRESENTER 5: How can we
re-imagine what a school should

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.770
be in the 21st century?

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.100
PRESENTER 6: We have empathy
building community service.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.141
PRESENTER 5: What is the
role of online learning?

00:00:37.141 --> 00:00:38.580
What is the role
of collaboration?

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.576
PRESENTER 7: So just discuss
and vote if we need to.

00:00:41.576 --> 00:00:43.200
PRESENTER 8: How
would we come together

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.180
as people from very
diverse backgrounds

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.206
and think about
solutions for education?

00:00:49.206 --> 00:00:52.330
PRESENTER 9: And we have
professors, teachers, students,

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:52.890
children.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.010
We have policymakers.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.340
PRESENTER 5: The stakes are
changing so fast that educators

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.220
have not been able to keep up.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.575
PRESENTER 10: When you can
break it down and tell it

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:04.165
to a five-year-old
or a 10-year-old,

00:01:04.165 --> 00:01:06.036
like the ones that
we have here today,

00:01:06.036 --> 00:01:08.670
then that is when you
really know your material.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.790
We had specific
workshop modules.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.440
I would teach them
a thing or two.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.640
They would go back into their
teams and implement that.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.450
PRESENTER 2: It's a really
interactive process.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.496
It's a very proactive process.

00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:23.680
It really forces you to think
in ways that you may not

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.377
have thought before.

00:01:25.377 --> 00:01:26.960
PRESENTER 11: Because
if you have TV--

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:27.685
PRESENTER 5: Design
thinking is really

00:01:27.685 --> 00:01:29.700
a kind of blending of
the scientific method

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:30.760
and the creative process.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.470
PRESENTER 9: And we're
going to have things inside.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.553
PRESENTER 12: What's great
about it is they really

00:01:34.553 --> 00:01:37.160
breakdown to these such
simple actionable steps.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.290
PRESENTER 13: Starts
with some brainstorming,

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.294
goes through some
iterations of like, OK,

00:01:41.294 --> 00:01:42.710
can we narrow down
all these ideas

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.190
that we're coming up with?

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.380
PRESENTER 5: So you have
a kind of observation,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.980
empathy, research, try to
frame the problem, work

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.480
through a series
of prototypes which

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.270
are equivalent to
the experimentation.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.436
And then test those results
and see if they match up

00:01:54.436 --> 00:01:57.224
with the hypothesis.

00:01:57.224 --> 00:02:01.040
PRESENTER 14: [LAUGHS]
Oh, that's awesome.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.330
PRESENTER 2: We are pitching
an online advising platforms

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.900
for students in lower income
neighborhoods who may or may

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:13.320
not have access to quality
counseling and career and life

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.069
advice services.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:17.360
PRESENTER 15: There's this
huge interest in food trucks

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.840
and science trucks.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.770
And a space where you could
track where it is and you could

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:22.715
rate it.

00:02:22.715 --> 00:02:24.840
How many of you have ever
eaten at a food truck?

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.310
PRESENTER 5: Everything that
we've been doing in two days

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.850
is going to be reduced
to two minutes.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.641
PRESENTER 15: For people
using and learning

00:02:29.641 --> 00:02:32.110
from these educational mobile
trucks, you need to know,

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.420
what will you learn?

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.400
What's the value of
learning this way?

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.350
And what are reliable reviews
of the best trucks out there?

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:42.420
AUDIENCE: So are you considering
any ed service as a truck?

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.540
PRESENTER 15: We're sort
of anticipating a market

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.400
for ed mobile trucks, but--

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.930
PRESENTER 10: The prizes
are reimbursable stipends

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.430
for your project development.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.110
Is the innovation creative?

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.240
Is it feasible?

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.360
Does it includes
multiple perspectives?

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.150
PRESENTER 16: It's a place
where teachers, students,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.620
and professionals who are
living in the community--

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.320
STUDENT: I'm hoping we win
because I would like to be

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.180
the youngest entrepreneur ever.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.180
That would be fun.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.890
PRESENTER 17: You all
uncovered some big themes.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.190
The need to collaborate.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.900
PRESENTER 18: I like
how much every group

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.190
got a real good handle
on the design process.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.340
PRESENTER 19: I thought
the energy was great.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.000
I really enjoyed the creativity.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.500
PRESENTER 20: And I am
a convert to trucks.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.400
I mean, I love food trucks,
but there are so many trucks.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.630
So basically, there are
mobile, physical things

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.890
that are going to
happen in education.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.140
I learned that this afternoon.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.040
Thank you all.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.490
[APPLAUSE]

